#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:167
msgid "Remove spaces: `strip`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:169
msgid "[`strip()`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.strip) removes spaces (and tabs and new lines) at **both ends**; [`lstrip()`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.lstrip) only at the start, [`rstrip()`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.rstrip) only at the end:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:180
msgid "**Strip given characters.** With an argument, [`strip`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.strip) removes **those characters** from the ends (any of them, in any order) - not a word:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:190
msgid "Spaces inside: `COMPBL` in SAS"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:192
msgid "In SAS, `COMPBL` (\"compress blanks\") replaces every group of spaces inside a text with one space. [`strip`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.strip) does not touch the spaces **inside** a text. [`split()`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.split) without an argument cuts the text into words at every group of spaces, and `\" \".join(...)` puts the words back with one space between them:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:204
msgid "`\" \".join(s.split())` is the usual Python way to say \"remove all extra spaces\". Section 4 explains [`split`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.split) and [`join`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.join)."
msgstr ""

