#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:214
msgid "Is it in there?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:226
msgid "**Several prefixes at once.** [`startswith`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.startswith) and [`endswith`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.endswith) also take a **tuple** of options - \"starts with one of these\":"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:237
msgid "Where is it? `find` and `count`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:239
msgid "[`find`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.find) gives the position of the first match, or **-1** if there is none. [`count`](https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str.count) says how many times a text occurs:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:250
msgid "The SAS functions `INDEX` and `FIND` count from **1** and give **0** when nothing is found. Python counts from 0 and gives -1. A classic bug: `if s.find(\"x\"):` is true for -1, and false when `\"x\"` is at position 0. Use `in` for \"is it there?\"."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:254
msgid "What kind of characters?"
msgstr ""

