msgid "More functions of `re`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1821
msgid "**[`finditer`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.finditer): the match objects.** [`finditer`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.finditer) gives the **match objects**, with their positions - when you need more than the text:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1832
msgid "**[`split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.split) with a pattern.** [`re.split`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.split) cuts at every match - when the separators vary:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1841
msgid "**`re.MULTILINE`.** With a text of many lines, `^` and `$` mean the start and the end of the **whole** text. The flag `re.MULTILINE` (`re.M`) makes them work **per line**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1856
msgid "**[`re.compile`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.compile): a pattern used many times.** [`re.compile`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.compile) turns a pattern into an object with the same methods. Give it a name at the top of the script, and use it in a loop - it reads well and is checked once:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1873
msgid "**[`re.subn`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.subn): count the replacements.** [`re.subn`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.subn) works like [`re.sub`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.sub), and also returns the number of replacements - useful in a cleaning report:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1883
msgid "**[`re.escape`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.escape).** When a text that you look for has special characters, [`re.escape(text)`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html#re.escape) puts the backslashes for you:"
